slang: two adjacent macro pastes paste once

"macro operators may only be used within a macro definition" on code other
tools accept: `a````b` in a macro body, which CORE-V-VERIF uses to build
names from an argument that may be empty (`rvfi_csr_``n````idx````sfx``_if`).
Pasting the left token with the second `` lexed as two tokens, the name and
the ``, so the `` stayed in the expansion. Now a paste followed directly by
another is skipped, and the second joins the left token with what follows.

Local issue SL-3 (patches/slang/README.md). Not reported upstream yet.
Present in v11.0 and on master (2026-10-16). Applies to v11.0.

--- a/source/parsing/Preprocessor_macros.cpp
+++ b/source/parsing/Preprocessor_macros.cpp
@@ -176,6 +176,17 @@
         // Paste together previous token and next token; a macro paste on either end
         // of the buffer or one that borders whitespace should be ignored.
         // This isn't specified in the standard so I'm just guessing.
+        //
+        // Two pastes with nothing between them (`a````b`, or an empty argument
+        // between two pastes) paste once, as other tools do: skip this one and
+        // let the next paste join the previous token with what follows it.
+        // Pasting the next `` itself would leave it in the expansion.
+        if (index + 1 < inputTokens.size() &&
+            inputTokens[index + 1].kind == TokenKind::MacroPaste && token.trivia().empty() &&
+            inputTokens[index + 1].trivia().empty() && emptyArgTrivia.empty()) {
+            return false;
+        }
+
         if (index == 0 || index == inputTokens.size() - 1 || !token.trivia().empty() ||
             !inputTokens[index + 1].trivia().empty() || !emptyArgTrivia.empty()) {
 
